  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Mostly dry with continued fall temperatures. An\nearly look at the holiday weekend favors dry weather, though \nlate Sunday into Monday bears watching for rain.\n\nTurning cooler tonight into Tuesday night, with below normal \ntemperatures expected. Patchy frost will be possible tonight \ntowards sheltered areas of the east slopes of the Berkshires. \nWinds will be too high to permit much frost. This should not be \nthe case for Tuesday night. Thinking more widespread \nopportunities for frost across the normally colder locations of \nMA and northern CT. \n\nA quiet stretch of fall weather is expected Wednesday and beyond\nas as high pressure remains the dominant feature. A vertically \nstacked low near Hudson Bay will send a few subtle shortwaves \nthrough New England Thursday and Friday. Limited moisture should\npreclude significant rainfall, though a spot shower cannot be \nruled out. Otherwise, dry weather prevails through the start of \nthe holiday weekend. Forecast uncertainty increases late Sunday \ninto Columbus Day and will depend largely on potential tropical \ndevelopment over the Bay of Campeche. The NHC currently gives \nAL92 an 80 percent chance of formation over the next 7 days. \nSome guidance brings remnant tropical moisture north into \nsouthern New England, while other solutions keep it suppressed \nto our south. Plenty of time to watch trends, with no reason to \nalter outdoor plans at this point. Temperatures remain fall-like\nwith highs mainly in the lower to middle 60s, except upper 60s \nto lower 70s Thursday. Overnight lows are mostly in the upper \n40s and 50s, though cooler upper 30s to middle 40s are possible \nSaturday night.",
